/* =====================================================================
   DAASI-3640 | 30b: the UPDATE. For the role that can update
   DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT and read its two streams.
   (Rev 2, 11/11 evening: narrowed. It adds the text that is missing
   and never replaces a comment with nothing.)

   What it does: on the comps that are missing text today, it writes the
   remarks (COMMENTS_EXTENDED, the new column) and, where the comp's own
   comment is not showing, the comment (COMP_COMMENTS_DESC). The list of
   those comps, with the text each one gets, is the work table
   DAASI3640_UPD_SCOPE that 30 part S builds, and step 1 counts it:
   1.2 to 1.3 million of 265 million are expected. The comps whose
   comment the new rule would only blank or shorten (about 6.6 million)
   are not in the run and keep what they show.
   It stamps UPDATED_DTTM, UPDATED_BY and LAST_DML_CD the way the load
   stamps an update. No other column is touched. MD5_CHECKSUM is left
   alone on purpose: the load compares it on its next touch of a comp
   and rewrites that row from raw. (It must never be set to NULL: the
   load would then skip the row for good.)

   When: the load task suspended with no run executing, trips has
   drained both streams on the fact, TRIP_ROOT_TASK kept from firing
   until step 3 is done, and 30 parts A, B and S run. Chris says when.
   The three steps go one after the other, without a pause.

   The task statements around it, as templates:
     ALTER TASK DAAS_PROD.DAAS_COMMON.RAW2CORE_HETCMS_COMP_DETAIL_FACT_TASK SUSPEND
     ALTER TASK DAAS_PROD.DAAS_COMMON.TRIP_ROOT_TASK SUSPEND
     (steps 1 to 3 below, then Chris runs 30 part C)
     ALTER TASK DAAS_PROD.DAAS_COMMON.TRIP_ROOT_TASK RESUME
     ALTER TASK DAAS_PROD.DAAS_COMMON.RAW2CORE_HETCMS_COMP_DETAIL_FACT_TASK RESUME
   ===================================================================== */

-- 1. Before. The rows the UPDATE will change. It must equal ROWS_THIS_RUN_UPDATES from 30 (S2),
--    and it shows that this role can read the list. If it answers "does not exist or not
--    authorized", the list's owner (DAAS_TEAM) grants the read:
--      GRANT SELECT ON TABLE DAAS_PROD.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI3640_UPD_SCOPE TO ROLE <this role>
SELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_to_update
FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI3640_UPD_SCOPE
WHERE IN_THIS_RUN;

-- 2. The UPDATE. One statement, one transaction. "number of rows updated" must equal
--    ROWS_TO_UPDATE. It finds each row by its own key and changes it only if it is still active
--    and still reads what the list saw, so it cannot touch a row that moved in between.
--    Use a Large. Run it once.
UPDATE DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT f
SET COMP_COMMENTS_DESC = s.TO_COMMENT,
    COMMENTS_EXTENDED  = s.TO_REMARKS,
    UPDATED_DTTM       = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    UPDATED_BY         = CURRENT_USER,
    LAST_DML_CD        = 'U'
FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI3640_UPD_SCOPE s
WHERE f.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_SK = s.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_SK
  AND s.IN_THIS_RUN
  AND f.DELETE_IND = 'N'
  AND f.COMP_COMMENTS_DESC IS NOT DISTINCT FROM s.OLD_COMMENT
  AND f.COMMENTS_EXTENDED  IS NOT DISTINCT FROM s.OLD_REMARKS;

-- 3. The two streams emptied, straight after step 2. The UPDATE has put every changed row into
--    both, and nothing else is in them: the load is suspended and trips had drained them. This
--    is Snowflake's own way of moving a stream past what it holds: a statement that reads the
--    stream and keeps nothing. The stream, its owner and its grants stay as they are.
--    (Recreating the two streams does the same job. If done that way: CREATE OR REPLACE STREAM
--    ... COPY GRANTS, run as the role that owns the stream today, because the role that
--    recreates a stream becomes its owner and COPY GRANTS does not carry ownership.)
CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY TABLE DAAS_PROD.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI3640_UNUSED_COMP_DOMAIN AS
SELECT * FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_COMP_DOMAIN_STREAM WHERE 1 = 0;

CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY TABLE DAAS_PROD.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI3640_UNUSED_FP_DOMAIN AS
SELECT * FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_FP_DOMAIN_STREAM WHERE 1 = 0;

--    Both streams now read 0. Then Chris runs 30 part C, and the tasks are resumed after it.
SELECT 'COMP_DOMAIN' AS STREAM, COUNT(*) AS ROWS_WAITING
FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_COMP_DOMAIN_STREAM
UNION ALL
SELECT 'FP_DOMAIN', COUNT(*)
FROM DAAS_PROD.DAAS_CORE.COMP_DETAIL_FACT_FP_DOMAIN_STREAM;